# Template for `dev.env` — the local development run: fast unoptimized
# debug builds for the Rust services, and a hot-reloading frontend.
#
#   cp dev.env.example dev.env   # then fill in real values
#   docker compose --env-file dev.env up --build
#
# ###########################################################################
# # DUPLICATION WARNING — read before editing                               #
# #                                                                         #
# # This file and `local.env.example` are each SELF-CONTAINED: both carry   #
# # the complete variable set, including INTERNAL_TOKEN and every           #
# # RDM_*_API_KEY. The same secrets therefore live in TWO places            #
# # (`dev.env` and `local.env`), and they WILL drift if you only update one #
# # of them. When you rotate a key or change a credential, change it in     #
# # BOTH files.                                                             #
# #                                                                         #
# # This is the accepted cost of the two-file split: it buys env files that #
# # need no layering and a mode switch that is just `--env-file <file>`.    #
# ###########################################################################
#
# Only three groups of values differ from local.env.example: COMPOSE_FILE
# (which pulls in the dev override), RUST_LOG / NEXT_ALLOWED_DEV_ORIGINS,
# and the shortened poll intervals. Everything else is identical, deliberately.
#
# NONE of the RDM_*_BASE_URL values below are real Rail Data Marketplace
# endpoints. They are deliberately non-functional placeholders
# (`*.example.invalid`, which is reserved by RFC 2606 and guaranteed to
# never resolve) so this file can't be mistaken later for a real
# configuration. As of this plan's completion, none of the following gaps
# have been resolved with real RDM account data:
#   - Incidents feed: no confirmed base URL/endpoint path.
#   - TOCs feed: no confirmed base URL/endpoint path.
#   - Exact `x-apikey` auth header name: corroborated only via a different
#     RDM product's documented example, not proven for these three feeds.
#   - Stations feed JSON field casing: unconfirmed (see
#     crates/poller-stations/src/schema.rs).
#   - LDBWS (Live Departure Board) feed: no confirmed base URL/product-slug
#     segment; two variants seen in research, not reconciled.
#   - LDBWS feed rate limit: one low-confidence source claims a number,
#     not corroborated against RDM's own plan/quota page.
# Fill in the real values only once you have an actual RDM account and have
# confirmed each endpoint against the live API.
#
# Consequence for a first run: the four pollers will fail against these
# placeholder hosts and restart in a loop — and with the shortened
# intervals below they will do so noisily. That is expected, not a
# regression; postgres, api, aggregator and frontend all come up fine.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compose wiring
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which YAML files `docker compose` loads. Compose reads COMPOSE_FILE out of
# the file passed to `--env-file` before it resolves the model, which is
# what lets a single `--env-file dev.env` select the whole mode: the base
# file plus the dev override (debug build args, the `runtime-dev` target,
# and the frontend bind mount that makes hot reload work). Separator is `:`.
COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.dev.yml
# To also bring up a local Authentik IdP for testing the SSO login flow
# with zero manual IdP-side setup, append ":docker-compose.authentik.yml"
# to the line above, i.e.:
#   COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.dev.yml:docker-compose.authentik.yml
# and set AUTHENTIK_SECRET_KEY (see docker-compose.authentik.yml's own
# header comment), then replace the SSO_* placeholders below with the local
# dev IdP's values -- see the OIDC section below for the exact values.
# See docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-29-dev-oidc-server-design.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# frontend dev server (frontend/next.config.mjs, docker-compose.dev.yml)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated list of origins `next dev` will accept cross-origin dev
# requests (HMR, the dev overlay, /_next/* assets) from. Next blocks
# unlisted ones and prints a warning naming the exact origin it wants —
# copy that value in here rather than guessing. Empty means "send no
# allowedDevOrigins at all": next.config.mjs omits the key entirely, which
# is the correct default for plain `http://localhost:3000` browsing.
#
# Verified on this stack: reaching the dev server on http://localhost:3000
# via the published port produced NO allowedDevOrigins warning, so empty is
# right unless you reach it by some other hostname or IP (across a LAN, or
# at the container's own bridge address).
NEXT_ALLOWED_DEV_ORIGINS=

# Dependency-change caveat: `frontend/node_modules` lives in an ANONYMOUS
# volume (see docker-compose.dev.yml) that Compose deliberately carries
# across recreates. After editing `frontend/package.json`, a plain
# `up --build` therefore leaves the container running the old deps. Use:
#   docker compose --env-file dev.env up --build --force-recreate -V frontend
# `-V` (--renew-anon-volumes) throws the volume away so Docker re-seeds it
# from the freshly built image.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# postgres
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_USER=distant_signal
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme-local-dev-only
POSTGRES_DB=distant_signal

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# api (crates/api/src/data/config.rs: ServiceArguments)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL and BIND_URL are assembled in docker-compose.yml from the
# postgres vars above / a fixed in-container bind address, so they aren't
# repeated here. API_HOST_PORT controls the host-side port mapping only.
API_HOST_PORT=8080

# Shared secret every poller must present via the `X-Internal-Token` header
# to reach the api's /private/* ingestion endpoints (crates/api/src/auth.rs).
# Must not be empty — the api refuses to start with an empty token.
INTERNAL_TOKEN=changeme-shared-secret-local-dev-only

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OIDC SSO configuration (crates/api/src/data/config.rs: ServiceArguments)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Point at a real or locally-run OIDC-compliant SSO server (e.g. Keycloak,
# Authentik, Authelia). All fields are required — and, like every other
# credential in this file, they carry non-empty `changeme-…` placeholders
# rather than being left blank. A blank value is strictly worse than a
# placeholder here: an empty-but-set env var still satisfies clap's
# "was it provided?" check, so the api gets all the way into
# `AppState::init` before failing its own `sso_client_secret` non-empty
# assertion (or `IssuerUrl::new("")` right after it), and the container
# crash-loops on a much less obvious error than "you didn't fill this in".
# `example.invalid` is RFC 2606-reserved and never resolves, matching the
# RDM_*_BASE_URL placeholders above — the api only parses this URL at
# startup (OIDC discovery is lazy), so the stack comes up fine and only an
# actual sign-in attempt fails until you point it at a real server.
# If you opted into the bundled local Authentik IdP above
# (docker-compose.authentik.yml), use these exact values instead of the
# changeme-* placeholders below -- they match
# authentik-blueprints/oauth2-client.yaml's fixed, deterministic client
# provisioning:
#   SSO_ISSUER_URL=http://authentik.localhost:9000/application/o/distant-signal/
#   SSO_CLIENT_ID=distant-signal-dev
#   SSO_CLIENT_SECRET=distant-signal-dev-local-only-not-a-real-secret
# SSO_REDIRECT_URL and SSO_POST_LOGIN_REDIRECT_URL below already match the
# blueprint's fixed redirect_uris and need no change.
SSO_ISSUER_URL=http://sso.example.invalid
SSO_CLIENT_ID=changeme-sso-client-id
SSO_CLIENT_SECRET=changeme-sso-client-secret
SSO_REDIRECT_URL=http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback
SSO_POST_LOGIN_REDIRECT_URL=http://localhost:3000/
SESSION_TTL_DAYS=14

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# poller-incidents (crates/poller-incidents/src/config.rs: Config)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GAP: RSPS5050 P-03-00 Rev A §10 does not publish an endpoint path for this
# product. Placeholder only — replace with the real feed base URL once known.
RDM_INCIDENTS_BASE_URL=http://rdm-incidents.example.invalid
RDM_INCIDENTS_API_KEY=changeme-rdm-incidents-api-key
# Recommended cadence per spec is 300s (5 min). Left at 300 even in dev —
# it is already short enough to watch.
POLL_INTERVAL_SECS_INCIDENTS=300

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# poller-stations (crates/poller-stations/src/config.rs: Config)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Confirmed path suffix per spec is `/stations`; the poller appends it
# itself. Host portion is account-specific — placeholder only.
RDM_STATIONS_BASE_URL=http://rdm-stations.example.invalid
RDM_STATIONS_API_KEY=changeme-rdm-stations-api-key
# Real-world cadence per RSPS5050 P-03-00 Rev A §6 is 86400s (24h, "once
# per day, overnight") — shortened here so the poll loop is actually
# visible in a dev session. Never carry this value into local.env.
POLL_INTERVAL_SECS_STATIONS=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# poller-tocs (crates/poller-tocs/src/config.rs: Config)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GAP: RSPS5050 P-03-00 Rev A §3 does not publish an endpoint path for this
# product in the current spec edition. Placeholder only.
RDM_TOCS_BASE_URL=http://rdm-tocs.example.invalid
RDM_TOCS_API_KEY=changeme-rdm-tocs-api-key
# Real-world cadence per spec is 86400s (24h, "at least once every 24
# hours") — shortened here for the same reason as stations above.
POLL_INTERVAL_SECS_TOCS=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# poller-ldbws (crates/poller-ldbws/src/config.rs: Config)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GAP: the exact RDM product-slug segment for the Live Departure Board
# product is unconfirmed — two variants were found during research
# (e.g. "1010-live-departure-board-dep" vs "...-dep1_2"). Placeholder only;
# confirm the real value once you have RDM account access.
LDBWS_BASE_URL=http://rdm-ldbws.example.invalid
LDBWS_API_KEY=changeme-rdm-ldbws-api-key
# Services requested per station per poll (LDBWS's own default is 10).
LDBWS_NUM_ROWS=10
# Target cadence per DESIGN.md is 30-60s; this feed's real rate limit is
# unconfirmed, so it stays at the conservative 60 even in dev — this is the
# one poller whose interval is NOT shortened.
POLL_INTERVAL_SECS_LDBWS=60

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# poller-tfl (crates/poller-tfl/src/config.rs: Config)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No GAP here: TfL's Unified API is public and documented, so the default in
# the binary is the real endpoint. Register at api-portal.tfl.gov.uk for a
# subscription key; the free tier is documented at ~500 requests/min, which
# one request every 300s is nowhere near.
TFL_BASE_URL=https://api.tfl.gov.uk
TFL_APP_KEY=changeme-tfl-subscription-key
# Line-status-only scope: no bus/river-bus/cable-car, and no national-rail
# (this app's own four pollers cover that far better than TfL's summary).
TFL_MODES=tube,dlr,overground,elizabeth-line,tram
# TfL publishes no recommended cadence; 300s mirrors poller-incidents.
# Shortened here so the poll loop is actually visible in a dev session —
# one request per cycle is still a rounding error against the documented
# ~500/min. Never carry this value into local.env.
POLL_INTERVAL_SECS_TFL=60

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# aggregator (crates/aggregator/src/config.rs: Config)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Target cadence per DESIGN.md is 30-60s (60 in local.env); 5 here so a
# change to the ingested data shows up on the dashboard almost immediately.
POLL_INTERVAL_SECS_AGGREGATOR=5
# How long line_status_history rows are kept before being pruned.
HISTORY_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# enricher (crates/enricher/src/config.rs: Config)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REDIS_URL is fixed to the in-network `redis://redis:6379` in
# docker-compose.yml, not overridden here.
# crates/enricher/src/config.rs -- point at a locally running
# OpenAI-compatible server (e.g. llama.cpp server, vLLM, Ollama).
LLM_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8080/v1
LLM_API_KEY=
# Model recommendation (2026-08-21 live-eval battery against a real
# self-hosted Ollama endpoint -- see
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-20-incident-nlp-extraction-design.md's
# "Live-eval results" section for the full comparison and methodology):
# gemma3:12b was the most reliable candidate tested -- correct multi-period
# segmentation, correctly resisted an over-segmentation trap, and was the
# only model with zero dropped/flipped fields across repeated identical
# calls. gpt-oss:20b is a reasonable fallback (slower, more run-to-run
# variance). Smaller models (qwen3.5:4b and under) were unreliable on this
# task in that eval -- your own model/backend may differ, so re-run that
# battery before trusting a different choice in production.
LLM_MODEL=replace-with-your-local-model-name
# Per-request LLM call timeout -- raise if extractions are timing out
# against a slow/remote server. If you raise this, raise
# RECLAIM_MIN_IDLE_SECS too (see its comment below): default shown.
# LLM_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECS=300
SWEEP_INTERVAL_SECS=300
# Debounced retry for a timed-out/failed extraction -- defaults shown.
# Keep RECLAIM_MIN_IDLE_SECS comfortably above 3 * LLM_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECS
# (one incident makes three sequential LLM calls).
# RECLAIM_INTERVAL_SECS=60
# RECLAIM_MIN_IDLE_SECS=1000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# trust-consumer (crates/trust-consumer/src/config.rs: Config)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GAP: none of these are confirmed against a live RDM Train Movements
# catalogue entry. Requires the RDM subscription/licence prerequisites in
# docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-28-train-tracking.md to be completed
# before these can be real values. There is no "real local Kafka"
# equivalent to this file's usual "point at something actually running
# locally" pattern until that prerequisite access exists, so these are the
# same placeholders as local.env.example -- trust-consumer will crash-loop
# on `docker compose up` until real values are supplied, same as every
# other GAP-flagged poller today.
KAFKA_BROKERS=kafka.example.invalid:9094
KAFKA_TOPIC=replace-with-confirmed-topic-name
KAFKA_CONSUMER_GROUP=distant-signal-trust-consumer
KAFKA_SASL_USERNAME=changeme-rdm-trust-consumer-key
KAFKA_SASL_PASSWORD=changeme-rdm-trust-consumer-secret
KAFKA_SASL_MECHANISM=PLAIN
REFERENCE_RELOAD_SECS=60
RETENTION_DAYS_TRAIN_EVENTS=90

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# frontend (frontend/lib/api.ts)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host-side port mapping only; API_BASE_URL itself is fixed to the
# in-network `http://api:8080` in docker-compose.yml, not overridden here.
FRONTEND_HOST_PORT=3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared across all pollers + api (log verbosity; tracing-subscriber
# EnvFilter syntax, e.g. "info", "poller_stations=debug", "warn").
# `debug` here — the whole point of dev mode.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUST_LOG=debug
